#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125348} :activity/id #uuid "f4bd8552-cbfe-5cdf-9c63-a1215aabe6f0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050453 :topic/id #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" :topic/math-academy-id 1907} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125762 :tutorial/content "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4274a05d-d402-5dfc-a339-e5a6b5a8e5b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3205 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c9024def-0495-5d67-911a-9b23824e8187" :step/math-academy-id 653353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124301 :question/id #uuid "69da2e7c-7ede-5966-8ff9-13bca51c0420" :question/math-academy-id "e-4813"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048176}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446401}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "e3832943-8321-5094-b97b-66a6951219cb" :step/math-academy-id 653361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124401 :question/id #uuid "7fed39de-7304-52a0-a94d-e55703175d7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380291}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "78c3b58c-ad85-58e8-8baf-c63bf9e544b1" :step/math-academy-id 653358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124402 :question/id #uuid "05151ae0-102c-5ce8-8bc6-082e22aa77d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446400}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "f647c92d-413b-597e-a1c7-89a867776426" :step/math-academy-id 653355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125858 :tutorial/content "Let's now look at an example of a subset of a vector space that is not a subspace.\n\nConsider the vector space $\\mathbb R_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ at+b \\: | \\: a,b  \\in\\mathbb{Z} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition. Indeed, suppose that $f(t)=a_1t+b_1$ and $g(t)=a_2t+b_2$ are two polynomials from $B.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since $a_1+a_2$ and $b_1+b_2$ are in $\\Bbb Z.$\n\n- $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication. As a counterexample, consider $f(t)=t$ and $k=0.5,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =0.5t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial from $B,$ because $0.5 \\notin \\Bbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is not a subspace of $\\mathbb R_2[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "532d5411-c233-58a0-88e3-bd13bc8fcd1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6193 :tutorial/title "Example of a Subset That Is Not a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "fa129907-91b4-5bfa-8c65-22954ea25d25" :step/math-academy-id 653354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125525}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124707}]}
